Subject: [PATCH] canvas: noise moves blended pixels and their alpha

The readback noise moved only colour, by one step, on any pixel at a
colour edge. An alpha-only hash or a hash of colours rounded to 16 steps
was then the same for every profile, and a canvas of solid rectangles
read back with changed edge pixels, which shows the noise.

Now only a pixel that is a blend of its neighbours moves: a colour none
of its eight neighbours has, inside the range they span. For 2D canvas
output the whole 3x3 neighbourhood must also lie on one line between two
colours in premultiplied space, which is what anti-aliasing an edge
between two colours leaves, in text and on curves; a grid of random
colours drawn pixel by pixel, as CreepJS draws to test getImageData,
never qualifies and reads back exactly. The pixel's colour and alpha
each move by up to two steps and stay inside its neighbours' range.
Solid fills, hard edges, straight half-pixel edges and lone dots read
back exactly as drawn. WebGL readPixels and WebGL canvas exports keep
the looser rule, as shader output is often noise with no two-colour
edges.

The step depends only on the pixel and its neighbours, not on where it
is, so one shape drawn at two places reads back the same. getImageData
reads a one-pixel border inside the canvas to decide the pixels at its
edges, so a read of part of a canvas agrees with a read of all of it and
with toDataURL and toBlob. A read that reaches past the canvas no longer
fills the outside with unset memory.

